</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-polar-volume-example">
<title>A Volume Computed in Polar Coordinates</title>

<example xml:id="example-polar-volume">
<title>Example III</title>
<statement>
<p>
Find the volume of the solid that lies under the paraboloid
<m>z = x^2 + y^2</m>, above the <m>xy</m>-plane, and inside the
cylinder <m>x^2 + y^2 = 2x</m>.
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<solution>
<p>
We notice that the cylinder can be re-written as
<m>(x-1)^2 + y^2 = 1</m>, which is shown in
<xref ref="fig-shifted-disk-prefigure"/>; the solid itself is shown in
<xref ref="fig-polar-volume-tikz"/>. In polar coordinates,
<m>x^2 + y^2 = 2x</m> becomes <m>r^2 = 2r\cos\theta</m>, that is,
<m>r = 2\cos\theta</m>, so the base disk is
</p>

<me>
D = \left\{(r,\theta)\ \middle|\
-\pi/2 \le \theta \le \pi/2,\ 0 \le r \le 2\cos\theta\right\}.
</me>

<p>
Therefore,
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
V = \iint_D \left(x^2 + y^2\right)dA
\amp= \int_{-\pi/2}^{\pi/2}\!\!\int_0^{2\cos\theta}
r^2\, r\,dr\,d\theta
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= \int_{-\pi/2}^{\pi/2}
\left[\frac{r^4}{4}\right]_0^{2\cos\theta} d\theta
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= 4\int_{-\pi/2}^{\pi/2}\cos^4\theta\,d\theta
= 8\int_0^{\pi/2}\cos^4\theta\,d\theta
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= 8\int_0^{\pi/2}\left(\frac{1+\cos 2\theta}{2}\right)^{2} d\theta
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= 2\int_0^{\pi/2}\left[1 + 2\cos 2\theta
+ \tfrac12\left(1 + \cos 4\theta\right)\right]d\theta
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= 2\left[\tfrac32\theta + \sin 2\theta
+ \tfrac18\sin 4\theta\right]_0^{\pi/2}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= 2\left(\frac{3}{2}\right)\left(\frac{\pi}{2}\right)
= \frac{3\pi}{2}.
</mrow>
</md>
</solution>

<figure xml:id="fig-shifted-disk-prefigure">
<caption>
The base of the solid in <xref ref="example-polar-volume"/>: the disk
<m>(x-1)^2 + y^2 \le 1</m>, whose boundary is the polar curve
<m>r = 2\cos\theta</m>.
</caption>
