</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-polar-gaussian-example">
<title>An Integral That Needs Polar Coordinates</title>

<example xml:id="example-polar-gaussian">
<title>Example IV</title>
<statement>
<p>
Calculate the double integral
<m>\displaystyle\iint_R e^{x^2+y^2}\,dx\,dy</m>, where <m>R</m> is the
unit disk centered at the origin.
</p>

<p>
<em>Hint:</em> Notice that you cannot solve this integral using
Cartesian coordinates.
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<solution>
<p>
In polar coordinates the unit disk is
<m>0 \le r \le 1</m>, <m>0 \le \theta \le 2\pi</m>, and
<m>e^{x^2+y^2} = e^{r^2}</m>. The extra factor of <m>r</m> in
<m>dA = r\,dr\,d\theta</m> is exactly what makes the integral
computable, via the substitution <m>u = r^2</m>:
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
\iint_R e^{x^2+y^2}\,dx\,dy
\amp= \int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^1 e^{r^2}\,r\,dr\,d\theta
= \int_0^{2\pi}\left[\tfrac12 e^{r^2}\right]_0^1 d\theta
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= \int_0^{2\pi}\tfrac12(e - 1)\,d\theta
= \pi(e-1).
</mrow>
</md>
</solution>

</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-polar-questions">
<title>Concept Check</title>

<p>
Each question below asks for a <em>setup</em> in polar coordinates. Recall
that <m>x = r\cos\theta</m>, <m>y = r\sin\theta</m> and
<m>dA = r\,dr\,d\theta</m>, so that
</p>

<me>
\iint_R f(x,y)\,dx\,dy
  = \iint_R f\left(r\cos\theta, r\sin\theta\right) r\,dr\,d\theta .
</me>

<p>
The work is almost entirely in the limits: which values of <m>\theta</m>
sweep the region, and for each such <m>\theta</m>, between which two
values of <m>r</m> does the ray at that angle enter and leave the region.
Two of the figures below carry a slider for <m>\theta</m> so you can watch
the ray move.
</p>

<exercise xml:id="q-polar-three-areas">
<title>Three areas</title>
<statement>
<p>
Set up <term>three</term> (not more than three) integrals that correspond
to the following areas graphed in <xref ref="fig-polar-two-areas"/>:
</p>
<ol>
<li><p>the red-colored area,</p></li>
<li><p>the blue-colored area,</p></li>
<li><p>the sum of the areas in (1) and (2).</p></li>
</ol>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>
Both regions are swept by the rays with <m>0 \le \theta \le \pi/3</m>: the
lower limit is the positive <m>x</m>-axis and the upper limit is the line
<m>y = \sqrt3\,x</m>. The vertical line <m>x = 1</m> is
<m>r\cos\theta = 1</m>, that is <m>r = \sec\theta</m>, and the two arcs
are <m>r = 1</m> and <m>r = 2</m>. Along a ray, the red region runs from
the unit circle out to the line <m>x = 1</m>, and the blue region takes
over from there out to the circle of radius <m>2</m>:
</p>
<md>
<mrow>
A_{\text{red}} \amp = \int_0^{\pi/3}\!\!\int_1^{\sec\theta} r\,dr\,d\theta
  = \frac{\sqrt3}{2} - \frac{\pi}{6} \approx 0.342 ,
</mrow>
<mrow>
A_{\text{blue}} \amp = \int_0^{\pi/3}\!\!\int_{\sec\theta}^{2}
  r\,dr\,d\theta = \frac{2\pi}{3} - \frac{\sqrt3}{2} \approx 1.228 ,
</mrow>
<mrow>
A_{\text{red}} + A_{\text{blue}} \amp
  = \int_0^{\pi/3}\!\!\int_1^{2} r\,dr\,d\theta = \frac{\pi}{2} .
</mrow>
</md>
<p>
The third integral is the point of the question: adding the two regions
removes the line <m>x = 1</m> from the picture, and what is left is the
annular sector between <m>r=1</m> and <m>r=2</m> over
<m>0 \le \theta \le \pi/3</m>, whose area is
<m>\frac12\left(2^2-1^2\right)\cdot\frac{\pi}{3} = \frac{\pi}{2}</m>. It
is a good check on the first two: <m>0.342 + 1.228 = 1.571 = \pi/2</m>.
</p>
</answer>
</exercise>

<figure xml:id="fig-polar-two-areas">
<caption>
Figure (1): two regions between the circles <m>r=1</m> and <m>r=2</m>,
cut apart by the vertical line <m>x=1</m> and swept by the angles
<m>0 \le \theta \le \pi/3</m>. The line <m>y=\sqrt3\,x</m> is the ray
<m>\theta = \pi/3</m>, and it meets <m>x=1</m> exactly on the outer
circle.
</caption>
