<exercise xml:id="q-polar-rose-mass">
<title>Mass of a piece of a rose</title>
<statement>
<p>
Region <m>R</m> is the region <term>inside</term>
<m>r=\cos(3\theta)</m> and <term>inside</term> <m>r=\frac12</m>. See
<xref ref="fig-polar-rose-circle"/>. Set up a double integral for
computing the mass of a planar object that occupies region <m>R</m> and
whose density is given by
<m>\rho(x,y)=\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}</m>.
</p>
<p>
<em>Hint</em>: note that as <m>\theta</m> varies, you may need to modify
the limits of <m>r</m> accordingly.
</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>
In polar coordinates the density is <m>\rho = 1/r</m>, and
<m>dA = r\,dr\,d\theta</m>, so the two factors of <m>r</m> cancel and the
integrand is <m>1</m>:
</p>
<me>
M = \iint_R \frac{1}{\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}\,dA
  = \iint_R \frac{1}{r}\; r\,dr\,d\theta
  = \iint_R dr\,d\theta .
</me>
<p>
Now the limits, which is where the hint bites. Take the petal around
<m>\theta = 0</m>, which is traced for
<m>-\frac{\pi}{6} \le \theta \le \frac{\pi}{6}</m>. Sliding the ray in
<xref ref="fig-polar-rose-circle"/> shows what happens: near
<m>\theta = 0</m> the petal is fatter than the circle, so the ray leaves
<m>R</m> at <m>r=\frac12</m>, while near the edges of the petal it is
thinner and the ray leaves at <m>r=\cos(3\theta)</m>. The changeover is
where <m>\cos(3\theta) = \frac12</m>, that is
<m>\theta = \pm\frac{\pi}{9}</m>. So for that petal
</p>
<me>
M_{\text{petal}}
 = \int_{-\pi/9}^{\pi/9}\!\!\int_0^{1/2} dr\,d\theta
 + 2\int_{\pi/9}^{\pi/6}\!\!\int_0^{\cos(3\theta)} dr\,d\theta ,
</me>
<p>
the factor <m>2</m> coming from the two symmetric ends of the petal. The
three petals are congruent, so
</p>
<me>
M = 3\,M_{\text{petal}}
  = \frac{\pi}{3} + 2 - \sqrt3 \approx 1.315 .
</me>
</answer>
</exercise>

<figure xml:id="fig-polar-rose-circle">
<caption>
Figure (2): the rose <m>r=\cos(3\theta)</m> and the circle
<m>r=\frac12</m>. Drag the slider to turn the ray of angle
<m>\theta</m>; the dots mark where that ray crosses each curve, which is
what fixes the limits of <m>r</m>.
</caption>
<interactive xml:id="int-polar-rose" platform="javascript" source="polar-widget.js"
             width="100%" aspect="10:11">
<slate surface="html">
<div data-widget="rose" style="text-align:center;
     font-family: var(--font-body, 'Open Sans', Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif);">
<canvas width="460" height="460"
        style="width:460px; max-width:100%; height:auto; border:1px solid #ddd;"></canvas>
<div style="margin-top:0.6em; display:flex; align-items:center;
            justify-content:center; gap:0.7em; flex-wrap:wrap;">
<label for="rose-theta" style="font-family: var(--font-headings, 'PT Serif', Times, serif);
       font-style:italic; font-size:1.2em;">&#952;</label>
<input id="rose-theta" type="range" min="0" max="48" value="0" step="1"
       style="width:60%; max-width:340px;"/>
</div>
<div class="pw-readout" style="margin-top:0.5em; line-height:1.5;"></div>
</div>
</slate>
<instructions>
<p>
Drag the slider to change <m>\theta</m>, which moves in steps of
<m>\pi/24</m>. The readout under the picture gives
<m>\cos(3\theta)</m> for the current ray, and the upper limit of
<m>r</m> for the region inside both curves: it is <m>\frac12</m> while
the ray is near the middle of a petal, and <m>\cos(3\theta)</m> near the
edges of a petal.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>

<exercise xml:id="q-polar-cylinder-sphere">
<title>The ball and the cylinder again, in polar</title>
<statement>
<p>
Set up a double integral (in polar coordinates) for computing the volume
of the region bounded by the cylinder <m>x^2+(y-1)^2=1</m> and the sphere
<m>x^2+y^2+z^2=1</m>. See <xref ref="fig-ws-cylinder-sphere"/>.
</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>
This is <xref ref="q-di-cylinder-sphere"/> again, and it is the reason
polar coordinates were worth developing. The height over a point of the
region of integration is still <m>2\sqrt{1-x^2-y^2} = 2\sqrt{1-r^2}</m>.
The two boundary circles are as friendly as they could be in polar form:
the sphere gives <m>r=1</m>, and the cylinder <m>x^2+(y-1)^2=1</m>
expands to <m>x^2+y^2 = 2y</m>, that is <m>r = 2\sin\theta</m>. They
cross where <m>2\sin\theta = 1</m>, at <m>\theta = \frac{\pi}{6}</m> and
<m>\theta = \frac{5\pi}{6}</m>.
</p>
<p>
For <m>\frac{\pi}{6} \le \theta \le \frac{5\pi}{6}</m> the sphere is the
binding constraint and the ray leaves at <m>r=1</m>; outside that range
the cylinder is, and the ray leaves at <m>r = 2\sin\theta</m>. Using the
symmetry about the <m>y</m>-axis,
</p>
<md>
<mrow>
V \amp= 2\int_0^{\pi/6}\!\!\int_0^{2\sin\theta}
2\sqrt{1-r^2}\;r\,dr\,d\theta
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp\quad + \int_{\pi/6}^{5\pi/6}\!\!\int_0^{1}
2\sqrt{1-r^2}\;r\,dr\,d\theta
\ \approx\ 1.693 .
</mrow>
</md>
<p>
Compare the region of integration here with the one in
<xref ref="q-di-cylinder-sphere"/>: the same set, described by two
circles instead of by a square root.
</p>
</answer>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="q-polar-cardioid-overlap">
<title>The overlap of two cardioids</title>
<statement>
<p>
Set up integral(s) for computing the area of the <term>overlap</term> of
the two curves <m>r(\theta)=1+\cos\theta</m> and
<m>r(\theta)=1+\sin\theta</m>. See
<xref ref="fig-polar-cardioids"/>.
</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>
A point lies in the overlap when its <m>r</m> is at most <em>both</em>
<m>1+\cos\theta</m> and <m>1+\sin\theta</m>, so along each ray the region
runs from <m>r=0</m> out to the smaller of the two. The two cardioids
cross where <m>1+\cos\theta = 1+\sin\theta</m>, that is
<m>\theta = \frac{\pi}{4}</m> and <m>\theta = \frac{5\pi}{4}</m>, and
these are exactly the angles where the smaller of the two changes
identity. Sliding the ray in <xref ref="fig-polar-cardioids"/> makes this
visible: the readout shows which of the two radii is smaller.
</p>
<me>
A = \int_{-3\pi/4}^{\pi/4}\!\!\int_0^{\,1+\sin\theta} r\,dr\,d\theta
  + \int_{\pi/4}^{5\pi/4}\!\!\int_0^{\,1+\cos\theta} r\,dr\,d\theta .
</me>
<p>
Each piece contributes the same amount, by the symmetry of the picture
about the line <m>\theta = \frac{\pi}{4}</m>, and
</p>
<me>
A = \frac{3\pi}{2} - 2\sqrt2 \approx 1.884 .
</me>
</answer>
</exercise>

<figure xml:id="fig-polar-cardioids">
<caption>
Figure (4): the cardioids <m>r=1+\cos\theta</m> and
<m>r=1+\sin\theta</m>. Drag the slider to turn the ray of angle
<m>\theta</m>; the overlap is what the ray reaches before it leaves the
first of the two curves.
</caption>
<interactive xml:id="int-polar-cardioids" platform="javascript" source="polar-widget.js"
             width="100%" aspect="10:11">
<slate surface="html">
<div data-widget="cardioids" style="text-align:center;
     font-family: var(--font-body, 'Open Sans', Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif);">
<canvas width="460" height="460"
        style="width:460px; max-width:100%; height:auto; border:1px solid #ddd;"></canvas>
<div style="margin-top:0.6em; display:flex; align-items:center;
            justify-content:center; gap:0.7em; flex-wrap:wrap;">
<label for="card-theta" style="font-family: var(--font-headings, 'PT Serif', Times, serif);
       font-style:italic; font-size:1.2em;">&#952;</label>
<input id="card-theta" type="range" min="0" max="48" value="0" step="1"
       style="width:60%; max-width:340px;"/>
</div>
<div class="pw-readout" style="margin-top:0.5em; line-height:1.5;"></div>
</div>
</slate>
<instructions>
<p>
Drag the slider to change <m>\theta</m>, which moves in steps of
<m>\pi/24</m>. The readout gives both radii for the current ray and the smaller of the two, which is the upper limit of
<m>r</m> for the overlap. Watch it swap from one curve to the other as
the ray passes <m>\theta = \frac{\pi}{4}</m> and
<m>\theta = \frac{5\pi}{4}</m>.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>

</subsection>

</section>
